README-CIO -- the starred programs, and the five modules that serve them=======================================================================A star on a name in DOC/INDEX means the program needs one of Microware'sruntime trap modules: `cio' for nearly all, and for some `math',`math881', `csl' or `csl020'.All five are in CMDS, included with Microware's permission; SOURCES.txtrecords the exchange.  The starred programs run here with nothing tosupply.  The star tells you what a program depends on, not what it lacks.The list was measured, by running every program on a disk with the fivemodules taken away.  A `cio' string inside a binary is no guide: `vi'contains it and does not need the module, while `cyberwar', `gnuchess'and `g' lack it and do.WHY SO MANY-----------`cc -qixm' -- the C library in a shared trap handler rather than linkedinto every binary -- was the normal way to build for OS-9, and it is whya starred program is often a fraction of the size of a trap-free one.Much of this collection has been rebuilt trap-free (`-qm'); the rest areas their authors built them.A program that cannot find its trap handler stops at once with    **** Can't install trap handler ****It is not broken.  On this disk you should never see it; if you do, thefive modules are gone or the execution directory is not this disk's CMDS.USING YOUR OWN--------------You run OS-9, so you have these modules already, and you may prefer yourcopy -- a different edition, or one matched to your processor.1. Load it.  A resident module is used wherever the program runs from:       load /h1/CMDS/cio   That is your own OS-9's `load', on PATH from /h1/CMDS.  The same fixes   the message when you run from an execution directory holding no cio:   `load /dd/CMDS/cio'.  (Measured 2026-08-28: with the execution   directory moved to a directory holding no cio, `banner HI' stops with   the message; after `load /dd/CMDS/cio' in the same session it draws   its banner.)  The RTF Fortran programs get `os9lib' the same way --   DOC/README-FORTRAN.2. Replace the copies here:       copy /h1/CMDS/cio  /dd/CMDS/cio       copy /h1/CMDS/math /dd/CMDS/math   That disk then holds modules from your own system, so keep it for your   own use.`math' is needed only by programs that do floating point, and `math881'is its twin for a machine with a 68881 -- DOC/README-FLOATINGPOINT.DOC/README-RUNNING covers where to mount the collection.WITHOUT THE MODULES-------------------Someone redistributing a subset, or testing what stands alone, may removeall five.  Most programs still run; the starred ones stop with the messageabove until a `cio' is put back.  Trap-free alternatives already here:    starred           use instead    -------           -----------    *ed  *emacs       vi, stevie    *ksh              bash, sh, gshell    *zoo              lha, tar, gzip    *fgrep            grep    *ckermit          kermit    *cat              less, more, headDOC/INDEX marks every one, so you can tell before you try.A FEW cio PROGRAMS FAIL EVEN WITH cio PRESENT---------------------------------------------What you see.  Loudly: hundreds of lines of the emulator's    No more memory !!!and no work done.  Since 2026-08-31 os9exec counts refused allocationsinstead of printing each one, so such a program is now silent and neverreturns.  Or quietly: the program opens your file, reads not one byte ofit, and reports on it anyway -- a three-line file "MAXLINECOUNTexceeded", eighteen bytes "word too long in line 0", or no output at all.Each looks like a small bug rather than a program that never read itsinput.  The usage text is always fine, because printf, fprintf and fwriteare unaffected; only the `putc' and `getc' macros on a FILE go wrong.The starred programs pass 272,000 bytes of input through with no trouble.`xrf', before its rebuild, showed it in one run:    I$Open  "cdiff.c"     <- the source, 30K    I$Open  "C.XRF"       <- the language table    I$Read  the table     and it printed "Language table loaded"    ...and cdiff.c was never read.The table went through read() and arrived.  The source went through the`getc' macro and never did.A single `No more memory !!!' can be the ordinary thing: the program askedfor memory and there was none.  What identifies this defect is a fileopened and never read.Why.  A cio program reaches the C library through TRAP #13 with aselector.  These programs were linked against a `cio.l' whose table has`_flshbuf' at selector $41; every `cio' module anyone has -- ours, theSDK's -- puts a memory routine there.  So `putc' asks for "flush thisFILE" and gets "allocate this many bytes", with the FILE's address as thecount.  The allocation succeeds, the FILE is never updated, and the nextcharacter does it again.  The cio module that would match these binariesis on none of the disks we have.Which.  Two versions of `cio.l' exist, and a binary shows which it linked:the highest trap-13 selector in its own stub table.  Every affectedprogram is from the $44 group; none from the $45 group is.  The moduleshere dispatch $00..$45, so the $45 library is the matching one.  In it,`putc' and `getc' are calls handled inside the module; in the $44 librarythey are macros whose slow path calls the mismatched selectors.What to do.  No amount of memory helps and the binary cannot be repaired,but the program usually can: a `-qm' build from the same source neverenters cio.  Twenty have been rebuilt that way, `cc <src> -qm=16k -DOSK-n=<name>', and every one works:    cvtbase   logisim   unstr   cdiff   pagekwic   pagefraz    nroff     etags     cookhash   yacc   xrf    printf    valspeak  unifdef   ape    hexed   vis    diff      crypto    unpacklibEach is larger for it -- `cvtbase' went from 4,666 bytes to 16,476 --which is the trade `-qixm' was making.  `cvtbase d h' now answers `ff'for 255; `cdiff' produces a context diff; `pagekwic' and `pagefraz' builda keyword-in-context index; `etags' writes a tags file; `yacc' writesy.tab.c; `nroff' formats.  `xrf' wants its language table `C.XRF' in thedata directory (it is here as DOC/xrf/c.xrf), and `logisim' wants the`PORT' environment variable set.  For the rest, use an alternative fromthe table above.Carrying the call is not making it.316 modules here link cio; 18 contain the call that can do this.  Thescan is a loose upper bound: a call site in a function nothing callscannot fire, and most are in error paths.  Run with real arguments andfiles, `ascii' writes its whole 4,290-byte table,`spiff' produces a diff, `strfile' builds a fortune index, `makelex'compiles a word list, `loan' prints a full amortisation schedule, and`snap', `lp', `undel', `dam', `newsgen', `blackjak', `poker', `stone',`tess' and `rpoem' all answer properly.  Do not read a name off the scanand conclude a program is broken; run it.The other direction is a trap too.  `setfont' writes no byte for a realfont file, to /term, to $PORT or to a file $PORT names, and never reachesits own `Can't open' message.  Its `-qm' build does exactly the same, sothe mismatch is not the cause.  Silence is not a diagnosis; a rebuildthat changes nothing is the cheapest way to tell, and worth doing beforereplacing an archive binary with one of ours.Relinking for size.  `tools/rebuild/relink_cio.sh' turns a `-qm' buildback into a `-qixm' one, and `-qixm' links the SDK's `cio.l' -- the $44library.  A relinked program that runs a putc/getc macro breaks exactlylike these.  `tools/cio_macro_scan.py' is the gate: relink where it issilent about the result, and build `-qm' where it is not.The eighteen, most call sites first.  They carry the call and may neverrun it, so this is a list to be careful with, not a list of brokenprograms:    CMDS/liborder           CMDS/loan               CMDS/makelex    CMDS/GAMES/newsgen      CMDS/chksum             CMDS/dam    CMDS/snap               CMDS/spiff              CMDS/GAMES/blackjak    CMDS/GAMES/poker        CMDS/GAMES/stone        CMDS/ascii    CMDS/undel              CMDS/GAMES/tess         CMDS/lp    CMDS/setfont            CMDS/strfile            CMDS/GAMES/rpoemIf you meet it in a program not named here, that is the shape torecognise: usage text fine, real work impossible, and the emulator's voicein place of the program's.tools/cio_macro_scan.py regenerates this list, and check_disk fails ifthe figures above stop matching the disk.